 2. IMF: Global economy nearing &quot;limits of resilience&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The IMF and World Bank annual meetings in Bangkok, Thailand today. Photo: Sirachai Arunrugstichai/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Global policymakers and central bankers are gathering in Bangkok this week for the International Monetary Fund and World Bank's annual meetings. 

 New economic forecasts warning of risks to the world economy's resilience will set the backdrop for discussions. Driving the news: Global growth has defied expectations, holding up despite the Middle East war and soaring oil prices. 

 But the IMF warns that dwindling energy reserves, mounting debt and growing reliance on the AI boom leave the world vulnerable to another shock. What they're saying: "The global economy has been resilient, but that resilience is reaching its limits," IMF economic counselor Silvana Tenreyro said at a press conference. 

 "Technology-related investment, while an important boon against the energy shock, is concentrating risks — making the global expansion heavily reliant on a small number of firms, sectors, and economies," Tenreyro wrote in a separate blog post . The intrigue: The AI boom has helped offset the economic toll of the energy shock, but it is also concentrating risks.

 An AI bust could leave global GDP 1.1% below the IMF's baseline forecast by 2028, with a larger 1.8% hit to the U.S. But stronger AI investment and productivity gains could lift global output 0.6% above baseline by 2029. By the numbers: The IMF largely maintained its global growth outlook in its latest World Economic Outlook released last night . It now expects inflation to stay hotter for longer.

 Global growth is projected at 3.1% in 2026, a slight upgrade from July but unchanged from April. The IMF expects growth to pick up to 3.4% in 2027. The U.S. economy is expected to grow 2.3% in both years, the fastest among G7 economies. Global inflation is forecast at 4.7% this year and 4.1% next year, both upward revisions from April, though only the 2027 forecast was raised since July. 
 
